{"records":[{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4r55hvz87r","title":"Negative DNA supercoiling induces genome-wide Cas9 off-target activity. Newton MD et al","description":"CRISPR/Cas9 is a powerful gene-editing technology, however off-target activity remains an important consideration for therapeutic applications. We have previous-ly shown that force stretching DNA induces off-target activity and hypothesised that distortions of the DNA topology in vivo, such as negative DNA supercoiling, could reduce Cas9 specificity. Using single-molecule optical-tweezers, we demonstrate that negative supercoiling λ-DNA induces sequence-specific Cas9 off-target bind-ing at multiple sites, even at low forces. Using an adapted CIRCLE-seq approach, we detect over 10,000 negative supercoiling induced Cas9 off-target double-strand breaks genome wide, caused by increased mismatch tolerance. We further demon-strate in vivo that directed local DNA distortion increases off-target activity in cells and that induced off-target events can be detected during Cas9 genome-editing. These data demonstrate that Cas9 off-target activity is regulated by DNA topology in vitro and in vivo, suggesting that cellular processes, such as transcription and replication, could induce off-target activity at previously overlooked sites. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-03-12T08:20:22.495Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4r55hvz87r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4r55hvz87r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Matt","last_name":"Newton","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Molecular Biology","Single Molecule Imaging","Next Generation Sequencing","CRISPR/Cas9","Genome Editing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"33tm3dd9dw","title":"FAME-CRISPR: Improved CRISPR-Cas9 Genome Editing via HDAC Inhibition and Engineered Virus-Like Particle Delivery ","description":"Raw data deposited for \"FAME-CRISPR: Improved CRISPR-Cas9 Genome Editing via HDAC Inhibition and Engineered Virus-Like Particle Delivery\" by Djamshidi et al-Cell Reports Methods","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-25T15:53:30.358Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/33tm3dd9dw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/33tm3dd9dw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mahbod","last_name":"Djamshidi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Sanger Sequencing","Immunofluorescence"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tv9fngg2fx","title":"NDC80 status pinpoints mitotic kinase inhibitors as emerging therapeutic options in clear cell renal cell carcinoma","description":"Raw image of IHC staining and Western blot of NDC80 in ccRCC. migration and invasion images in HK2, 786-O and Caki-1 cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-03-16T08:53:35.852Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tv9fngg2fx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tv9fngg2fx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Cheng","last_name":"Hu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Research Article"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nc9bc8dn4m","title":"Single-Cell VIPER Identifies Recurrence-Associated Renal Tumor Macrophages","description":"single-cell RNASeq, IHC, flow cytometry, bulk RNASeq data, and code supporting the manuscript \"Tumor-Specific Cell Populations in Clear Cell Renal Carcinoma Associated with Clinical Outcome Identified Using Single-Cell Protein Activity Inference\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-05-12T09:40:12.027Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nc9bc8dn4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nc9bc8dn4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Aleksandar","last_name":"Obradovic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nivedita","last_name":"Chowdhury","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Scott","last_name":"Haake","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Casey","last_name":"Ager","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Vinson","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lukas","last_name":"Vlahos","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xinzheng","last_name":"Guo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"David","last_name":"Aggen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kim","last_name":"Rathmell","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Eric ","last_name":"Jonasch","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joyce","last_name":"Johnson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"James","last_name":"McKiernan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Brian","last_name":"Rini","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andrea","last_name":"Califano","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Charles","last_name":"Drake","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mark","last_name":"Roth","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Katy","last_name":"Backermann","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Renal Cell Carcinoma"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pv8t3hbg4t","title":"Proteasome-Bound UCH37 Debranched Ubiquitin Chains to Promote Degradation. Deol et al. ","description":"The following documents show sequencing data confirming CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing of cell lines, all gels used for analysis, and mass spectrometric chromatograms and LC traces. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-09T23:09:46.22Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pv8t3hbg4t","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pv8t3hbg4t"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Eric","last_name":"Strieter","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kirandeep","last_name":"Deol","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sean","last_name":"Crowe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jiale","last_name":"Du","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Heather ","last_name":"Bisbee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Robert","last_name":"Guenette","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mass Spectrometry","Gel","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"692c5jjpr2","title":"A clear cell renal cancer stem cell niche supported by autophagic methionine secretion from PDGFR-β+GPR91+ tumor-associated pericytes ","description":"Pericytes are heterogeneous mural cells surrounding blood vessels that are critically involved in malignant progression. Here we identify a subset of vascular pericytes, defined by expression of PDGFR-β and GPR91, that promote tumorigenesis and tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKIs) resistance by functioning as the primary methionine source for cancer stem cells (CSCs) in clear cell renal cell carcinoma (ccRCC). Tumor cell-derived succinate binds to GPR91 on the pericyte surface to activate autophagy for methionine production. CSCs use methionine to create stabilizing N6-methyladenosine (m6A) in ATAD2 mRNA, and the resulting ATAD2 protein complexes with SRY-box transcription factor 9 (Sox9) to establish super enhancers and thereby dictate its target genes that feature prominently in CSCs. Targeting PDGFR-β+GPR91+ pericytes with specific GRP91 antagonists effectively reduced intratumoral methionine level, eliminated CSCs and sensitized tumors to TKIs. Our results describe how PDGFR-β+GPR91+ pericytes provide supportive niche for CSCs and therefore could be potential targets for treating ccRCC.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-11-15T10:54:19.927Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/692c5jjpr2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/692c5jjpr2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Li","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"81874064; 82072692; 82272854"}],"categories":["Cancer Stem Cell","Tumor Metabolism","Cancer Epigenetics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9w4g54bv43","title":"Genome-wide screens reveal that resveratrol induces replicative stress in human cells","description":"Resveratrol is a natural product associated with wide-ranging effects in animal and cellular models including lifespan extension. To identify the genetic target of resveratrol in human cells, we conducted genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screens to pinpoint genes that confer sensitivity or resistance to resveratrol. An extensive network of DNA damage response and replicative stress genes exhibited genetic interactions with resveratrol and its analog pterostilbene. These genetic profiles showed similarity to the response to hydroxyurea, an inhibitor of ribonucleotide reductase that causes replicative stress. Resveratrol, pterostilbene and hydroxyurea caused similar depletion of nucleotide pools, inhibition of replication fork progression and induction of replicative stress. The ability of resveratrol to inhibit cell proliferation and S phase transit was independent of the histone deacetylase Sirtuin 1, which has been implicated in lifespan extension by resveratrol. These results establish that a primary impact of resveratrol on human cell proliferation is the induction of low-level replicative stress.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-04T14:56:19.488Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9w4g54bv43","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9w4g54bv43"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yahya","last_name":"Benslimane","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thierry","last_name":"Bertomeu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jasmin","last_name":"Coulombe-Huntington","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mary","last_name":"McQuaid","orcid":null},{"first_name":"María","last_name":"Sánchez-Osuna","orcid":null},{"first_name":"David","last_name":"Papadopoli","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daina","last_name":"Avizonis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mariana","last_name":"De Sa Tavares Russo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Caroline","last_name":"Huard","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ivan","last_name":"Topisirovic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hugo","last_name":"Wurtele","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mike","last_name":"Tyers","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lea","last_name":"Harrington","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Replication","High-Throughput Screening","High-Throughput Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mmnwww6n3s","title":"LZTFL1 Rewires NADPH–Glutathione Metabolism to Amplify Ferroptosis","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent form of regulated cell death, represents a therapeutic vulnerability in apoptosis-resistant cancers. Here, we identify leucine zipper transcription factor-like 1 (LZTFL1) as a key regulator of ferroptosis that rewires glutathione (GSH) metabolism. A genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screen and multi-omics analysis revealed that LZTFL1 promotes oxidation of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD), limiting NADPH production and GSH regeneration. GSH depletion in turn enhanced LZTFL1 translation via an AKT–mTOR–eIF4E pathway, establishing a feed-forward loop that amplifies ferroptosis. In vivo, the LZTFL1–FHOD1–G6PD axis sensitized xenograft and patient-derived xenograft models to ferroptosis, correlating with lipid peroxidation, reduced GSH, and extended survival. Clinically, LZTFL1 expression restored cisplatin efficacy in resistant lung and ovarian cancer cells and predicted better survival outcomes in LUAD patients. Moreover, FDA-approved compounds upregulated LZTFL1 and re-sensitized resistant tumors to cisplatin. These findings define LZTFL1 as both a biomarker and therapeutic target for enhancing ferroptosis-based cancer therapy. The files below contain raw western blot images and flow cytometry data from this study.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-12T06:59:22.445Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mmnwww6n3s","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mmnwww6n3s"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"XIANGFEI","last_name":"XUE","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Flowmetry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z4ht3txn2k","title":"Accompanying Files for paper entitled: \"Genome-scale mapping of DNA damage suppressors through phenotypic CRISPR/Cas9 screens\" - Zhao et al. Molecular Cell 2023","description":"Accompanying Files for paper entitled: \"Genome-scale mapping of DNA damage suppressors through phenotypic CRISPR/Cas9 screens\" - Zhao et al. Molecular Cell 2023. This dataset contains Source and additional Data for all figures.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-06T07:16:19.286Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z4ht3txn2k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z4ht3txn2k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yichao","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":null}],"categories":["DNA Replication","DNA Repair","Flow Cytometry","Cancer Chemotherapy","DNA Damage","Functional Genomics","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":135820,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":140,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":135820,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":135125,"restricted":68,"embargoed":627},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2242},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":683},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":464},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":427},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":426},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":412},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":385},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":329},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":275},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":225}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":133178},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1911},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":183},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":133},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida"